<section xml:id="sec-mod2-practice-set">
  <title>Module 2: Practice Problem Set</title>
  <p>Estimated time: 90 minutes</p>

    <p>
      Problems are labeled <m>[\mathbf{F}]</m> (Foundational),
      <m>[\mathbf{S}]</m> (Standard), and <m>[\mathbf{C}]</m>
      (Challenge). Work in order within each level; later problems
      in a level often build on earlier ones.
    </p>
    <p>
      These problems are not submitted, but you should write out
      complete solutions. For any problem that asks you to
      <em>prove</em> something, apply the standards from the Worked
      Examples: complete sentences, explicit quantifiers, every step
      justified.
    </p>

  <!-- ============================================================
       FOUNDATIONAL
  ============================================================ -->

  <paragraphs xml:id="par-mod2-ps-f">
    <title>Foundational Problems</title>
    <p>
      These problems practice the basic moves of the module: computing
      absolute values, rewriting absolute value inequalities as intervals,
      finding upper and lower bounds, and identifying suprema and infima.
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod2-ps-f1">
    <title><m>[\mathbf{F}]</m> Absolute Value Computations</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Compute each value. In each case, write out the definition
        of absolute value explicitly (i.e., note whether the
        argument is negative or nonnegative) before simplifying.
        <ol marker="(a)">
          <li><m>|7 - 11|</m></li>
          <li><m>|{-4} \cdot 3|</m></li>
          <li><m>\left|\dfrac{-6}{2}\right|</m></li>
          <li><m>|3 - \pi|</m> (leave in terms of <m>\pi</m>)</li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        (a) <m>|7 - 11| = |{-4}| = 4</m>.
        (b) <m>|{-12}| = 12</m>; alternatively <m>|{-4}| \cdot |3| = 4 \cdot 3 = 12</m>.
        (c) <m>|{-3}| = 3</m>.
        (d) Since <m>\pi \approx 3.14159 \gt 3</m>, we have
        <m>3 - \pi \lt 0</m>, so <m>|3 - \pi| = \pi - 3</m>.
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod2-ps-f2">
    <title><m>[\mathbf{F}]</m> Interval Translation</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Rewrite each absolute value inequality as an interval.
        Identify the center and radius of each interval.
        <ol marker="(a)">
          <li><m>|x - 5| \lt 3</m></li>
          <li><m>|x + 2| \leq 1</m></li>
          <li><m>|3x - 6| \lt 9</m></li>
          <li><m>|x - a| \lt \delta</m> (for <m>a \in \R</m> and
          <m>\delta \gt 0</m>)</li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        (a) <m>2 \lt x \lt 8</m>; center 5, radius 3.
        (b) <m>-3 \leq x \leq -1</m>; center <m>-2</m>, radius 1.
        (c) <m>|3(x-2)| \lt 9 \Rightarrow |x-2| \lt 3 \Rightarrow
        -1 \lt x \lt 5</m>; center 2, radius 3.
        (d) <m>a - \delta \lt x \lt a + \delta</m>; center <m>a</m>,
        radius <m>\delta</m>.
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod2-ps-f3">
    <title><m>[\mathbf{F}]</m> Identifying Bounds</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        For each set, determine whether it is bounded above, bounded
        below, or both. If it has an upper bound, give one; if it
        has a lower bound, give one. Do not yet find the exact supremum
        or infimum.
        <ol marker="(a)">
          <li><m>\left\{\dfrac{2}{n} : n \in \N\right\}</m></li>
          <li><m>\{x \in \R : x^2 \lt 4\}</m></li>
          <li><m>\{(-2)^n : n \in \N\}</m></li>
          <li>The interval <m>(0, 1]</m></li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        (a) Bounded above by 2 (at <m>n = 1</m>); bounded below by 0
        (all terms are positive). (b) Bounded: <m>x^2 \lt 4 \Rightarrow |x| \lt 2</m>,
        so <m>-2</m> is a lower bound and <m>2</m> is an upper bound.
        (c) The terms alternate sign and grow in magnitude
        (<m>-2, 4, -8, 16, \ldots</m>); neither bounded above nor below.
        (d) Bounded above by 1 and below by 0.
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod2-ps-f4">
    <title><m>[\mathbf{F}]</m> Identifying Suprema and Infima</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        For each set, identify <m>\sup S</m> and <m>\inf S</m>
        (if they exist). State whether the supremum or infimum
        is attained (i.e., belongs to <m>S</m>). Do not write
        a formal proof; a clear explanation is sufficient.
        <ol marker="(a)">
          <li><m>S = \left\{\dfrac{2}{n} : n \in \N\right\}</m></li>
          <li><m>S = \{x \in \R : x^2 \lt 4\}</m></li>
          <li><m>S = (0, 1]</m></li>
          <li><m>S = \left\{\dfrac{n}{n+1} : n \in \N\right\}</m></li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        (a) <m>\sup S = 2</m> (attained at <m>n = 1</m>);
        <m>\inf S = 0</m> (not attained; the terms approach 0).
        (b) <m>S = (-2, 2)</m>, so <m>\sup S = 2</m> and
        <m>\inf S = -2</m>, neither attained.
        (c) <m>\sup S = 1</m> (attained); <m>\inf S = 0</m> (not attained).
        (d) Terms are <m>1/2, 2/3, 3/4, \ldots</m>, increasing to 1;
        <m>\sup S = 1</m> (not attained); <m>\inf S = 1/2</m> (attained
        at <m>n = 1</m>).
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ============================================================
       STANDARD
  ============================================================ -->

  <paragraphs xml:id="par-mod2-ps-s">
    <title>Standard Problems</title>
    <p>
      These problems apply the same moves to longer arguments: proofs using
      the Triangle Inequality, bounding a product, and supremum proofs from
      the definition and from the <m>\varepsilon</m>-characterization.
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod2-ps-s1">
    <title><m>[\mathbf{S}]</m> A Triangle Inequality Application</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Prove that for any <m>a, b, c \in \R</m>,
        <md>|a - c| \leq |a - b| + |b - c|.</md>
        This is sometimes called the <em>metric inequality</em> for
        <m>\R</m>: the distance from <m>a</m> to <m>c</m> is at most
        the sum of the distances from <m>a</m> to <m>b</m> and from
        <m>b</m> to <m>c</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <hint>
      <p>
        Write <m>a - c = (a - b) + (b - c)</m> and apply the
        Triangle Inequality directly.
      </p>
    </hint>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod2-ps-s2">
    <title><m>[\mathbf{S}]</m> Bounding a Product</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Suppose <m>|x - 3| \lt 1</m>.
        <ol marker="(a)">
          <li>
            Use the Triangle Inequality (or Theorem 1.23(c)) to show that
            <m>|x + 3| \lt 7</m>. (Hint: write
            <m>x + 3 = (x - 3) + 6</m>.)
          </li>
          <li>
            Using part (a), prove that
            <m>|x^2 - 9| \leq 7 \cdot |x - 3|</m>.
            (Hint: factor <m>x^2 - 9 = (x-3)(x+3)</m> and use
            <m>|uv| = |u||v|</m>.)
          </li>
          <li>
            Conclude: if <m>|x - 3| \lt \min\!\left(1, \varepsilon/7\right)</m>,
            then <m>|x^2 - 9| \lt \varepsilon</m>.
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <hint>
      <p>
        For (a): <m>|x + 3| = |(x-3) + 6| \leq |x-3| + 6 \lt 1 + 6 = 7</m>.
      </p>
    </hint>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod2-ps-s3">
    <title><m>[\mathbf{S}]</m> Proving a Supremum with the <m>\varepsilon</m>-Characterization</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>T = \left\{\dfrac{n}{n+1} : n \in \N\right\}</m>.
        Prove that <m>\sup T = 1</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Your proof must follow the two-step structure from Worked
        Example 3: first show 1 is an upper bound, then use the
        <m>\varepsilon</m>-characterization to show that no smaller
        number is an upper bound. Include scratch work before the
        formal proof.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <hint>
      <p>
        To show <m>n/(n+1) \gt 1 - \varepsilon</m>, rearrange to
        find a condition on <m>n</m>, then use the Archimedean Property
        to guarantee such <m>n</m> exists.
      </p>
    </hint>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod2-ps-s4">
    <title><m>[\mathbf{S}]</m> Supremum of a Set Defined by an Inequality</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>U = \{x \in \R : x^2 \leq 5\}</m>.
        <ol marker="(a)">
          <li>
            Identify <m>\sup U</m> and <m>\inf U</m>. (No proof required;
            explain your reasoning in one or two sentences.)
          </li>
          <li>
            Prove your answer for <m>\sup U</m> by verifying
            both conditions in the definition of supremum.
            For condition (ii), note that <m>\sqrt{5} \in U</m>.
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ============================================================
       CHALLENGE
  ============================================================ -->

  <paragraphs xml:id="par-mod2-ps-c">
    <title>Challenge Problems</title>
    <p>
      These problems extend the module's ideas: uniqueness of the supremum,
      and a derivation of the Archimedean Property from the Completeness
      Axiom.
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod2-ps-c1">
    <title><m>[\mathbf{C}]</m> Uniqueness of the Supremum</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Prove that if <m>S</m> is a nonempty subset of <m>\R</m>
        and both <m>s</m> and <m>s'</m> satisfy the definition of
        <m>\sup S</m>, then <m>s = s'</m>. In other words, the
        supremum, if it exists, is unique.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <hint>
      <p>
        Since both <m>s</m> and <m>s'</m> are upper bounds of <m>S</m>,
        and both are least upper bounds, use the second condition of
        the definition (a supremum is at most every upper bound) to show <m>s \leq s'</m> and <m>s' \leq s</m>.
      </p>
    </hint>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod2-ps-c2">
    <title><m>[\mathbf{C}]</m> The Archimedean Property from Completeness</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Assume the Completeness Axiom. Prove the Archimedean Property:
        for every <m>x \in \R</m>, there exists <m>n \in \N</m>
        such that <m>n \gt x</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <!-- TODO(quinn): verify against the statement of Bauldry's Theorem 2.3. -->
        (This is the case <m>\varepsilon = 1</m> of Bauldry's Theorem 2.3,
        stated for every real <m>x</m> rather than only <m>x \gt 0</m>.
        Working through it yourself before the Bridge Reading will
        make Bauldry's proof much easier to follow.)
      </p>
    </statement>
    <hint>
      <p>
        Suppose, for contradiction, that no such <m>n</m> exists,
        i.e., that <m>x</m> is an upper bound for <m>\N</m>. Apply
        the Completeness Axiom to <m>\N</m> to obtain
        <m>\alpha = \sup \N</m>. Then consider <m>\alpha - 1</m>:
        use the <m>\varepsilon</m>-characterization with
        <m>\varepsilon = 1</m> to derive a contradiction.
      </p>
    </hint>
  </exercise>

</section>
